---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { label: "Branches and worktrees", href: "#worktrees" },
  { label: "Diffs", href: "#diffs" },
  { label: "Commit and push", href: "#commit" },
  { label: "Pull requests", href: "#prs" },
  { label: "GitHub sign-in", href: "#github" },
  { label: "Providers", href: "#providers" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Source control"
  description="Every Code thread can own a branch and a worktree. When the change is good, commit, push, and open a pull request from the same surface."
  currentPath="/docs/source-control"
  eyebrow="Product"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="worktrees">
    <h2>Branches and worktrees</h2>
    <p>
      A Code thread is Git work, not a chat that happens to mention files.
      When you start a thread you choose whether to use the current checkout
      or an isolated worktree on its own branch.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div>
        <strong>Current checkout</strong>
        <span>
          The agent edits the files you already have open. Fast, and dangerous
          if another person or agent is also writing.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Isolated worktree</strong>
        <span>
          Modesto creates a linked worktree so the thread can commit without
          disturbing your main working tree. Parallel agents stay out of each
          other’s uncommitted files.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Checkpoints are Git objects.</strong>
      <p>
        Handoffs pin <code>HEAD</code> and the working tree under{" "}
        <code>refs/modesto/agent-checkpoints/…</code>. They do not rewrite your
        branches. See <a href="/docs/checkpoints">Checkpoints</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="diffs">
    <h2>Diffs</h2>
    <p>
      Changed files stream into the thread. Open the diff beside the
      conversation, jump file to file, and treat the working tree — not the
      transcript — as the source of truth. If a check did not run, keep that
      fact in the checkpoint instead of assuming the tree is clean.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="commit">
    <h2>Commit and push</h2>
    <p>
      Git actions live on the thread: stage, commit, push, and open a pull
      request when the revision is ready. Generated commit messages are a
      starting point — edit them before you push anything you would not put
      on a shared branch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Push uses the git credentials already on the machine (SSH agent,{" "}
      <code>gh</code>, credential helper). Modesto does not mint a GitHub
      identity for you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="prs">
    <h2>Pull requests</h2>
    <p>
      When the branch has a coherent change, open a pull request from the
      thread. Modesto can draft a title and body from the work on that
      branch. Review the draft, choose draft vs ready, then create the PR on
      the host you authenticated.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open any pull request in Modesto to see its <strong>Context</strong>{" "}
      tab — threads that touched the branch, handoffs between them, and a
      one-click continue. That is the first surface of the{" "}
      <a href="/docs/context-graph">Context Graph</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="github">
    <h2>GitHub sign-in</h2>
    <p>
      Pull requests and GitHub identity use the real <code>gh</code> CLI on the
      host. Modesto does not host a GitHub OAuth app for you. In settings, Sign
      in with GitHub starts <code>gh auth login --web --clipboard</code>, shows
      the device code, and waits until that CLI session is authenticated.
    </p>
    <p>
      If <code>gh</code> is missing or signed out, GitHub actions fail with
      “Run <code>gh auth login</code> and retry.” Complete that on the same
      machine (or WSL/SSH environment) that owns the repo — not on a paired
      phone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="providers">
    <h2>Git hosts</h2>
    <p>
      Source control is not GitHub-only. Modesto speaks GitHub, GitLab,
      Azure DevOps, and Bitbucket through the CLIs and APIs already on your
      machine. Auth stays with those tools — typically <code>gh</code>,{" "}
      <code>glab</code>, or the host’s own login.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
